# {'requestId': 'd14d02ad86b7b3f8cd9dad34d0a5d937', 'type': 'text-delta', 'delta': '', 'choices': [{'index': 0, 'delta': {'role': 'assistant'}, 'finish_reason': None}]}
# {'requestId': 'd14d02ad86b7b3f8cd9dad34d0a5d937', 'type': 'results', 'results': [{'id': 'https://www.nestle.co.nz/ask-nestle/products-brands/answers/products-halal', 'title': 'Are any Nestlé products halal?', 'url': 'https://www.nestle.co.nz/ask-nestle/products-brands/answers/products-halal', 'highlights': ['#### Is*KitKat*halal certified?\n...\nOur plain milk chocolate KitKat range made in Australia is halal certified. This includes the 2 finger (17g), 4 finger (45g), ChunKy (50g), King Size (65g), and 170g block made in Australia, as well as share bags and bulk packs. As of April 2019, KitKat Gold, KitKat Chunky Caramel and KitKat Dark are also Halal certified.'], 'favicon': 'https://www.nestle.co.nz/themes/custom/da_vinci_code/images/favicons/favicon.ico'}, {'id': 'https://halalspy.com/halal-products/snacks/is-kitkat-halal/', 'title':'Is KitKat Halal? UK, Global and Nestlé Certification Explained | HalalSpy', 'url': 'https://halalspy.com/halal-products/snacks/is-kitkat-halal/', 'publishedDate': '2026-06-18T00:00:00.000Z', 'highlights': ["Yes for most UK varieties. Nestle UK has confirmed that KitKat is suitable for a halal diet, with numerous KitKat varieties listed on Nestle's official UK halal products list. All UK KitKat products are free from alcohol, gelatin, meat, fish, and their derivatives. KitKat Malaysia is JAKIM certified under a separate supply chain.\n...\nUK KitKat is suitable for a halal diet according to Nestle UK. On social media, KitKat’s official account confirmed: “Yes, our KitKats are suitable for a Halal diet.” Nestle UK & Ireland maintains an official halal products list (updated March 2025) that includes numerous KitKat varieties. All productson this list are free from alcohol, gelatin, meat, fish, and their derivatives.\n...\nWhile KitKat does not carry a formal HFA (Halal Food Authority) or HMC (Halal Monitoring Committee) certification stamp on UK packaging, Nestle’s own verification and halal product listing provides assurance that the ingredients and production process meet halal requirements. KitKat Malaysia, manufactured by Nestle Malaysia, is JAKIM (Jabatan Kemajuan Islam Malaysia) certified under a separate product formulation and supply chain.\n...\nNestlé Malaysia: JAKIM halal certified. JAKIM is Malaysia’s Department of Islamic Development, one of the most respected halal certification bodies in the world. KitKat produced at Nestlé’s Malaysian facilities carries the JAKIM logo.\n...\nreferenced by JAKIM’\n...\nofficial halal database.\n...\nUK (Nestlé UK, York factory): Neither HFA nor HMC has certified KitKat produced for the UK market. The HFA’s published certified product list does not include standard Nestlé KitKat UK lines. Nestlé UK has not made any public statement committing to UK halal certification as of 2025.\n...\nIs KitKat halal in the UK? No. KitKat sold in the UK is not halal certified. Nestlé’s UK production facility in York holds no HFA or HMC halal certification as of 2025. The ingredients do not contain pork, but the lack of certification and the presence of natural vanilla flavouring (which may use ethanol extraction) make UK KitKat unsuitable for Muslims who require certified halal products.\n...\nIs KitKat Malaysia halal? Yes. KitKat produced by Nestlé Malaysia is JAKIM halal certified. JAKIM is Malaysia’s federal Islamic authority responsible for halal certification. This certification covers KitKat products manufactured at Nestlé Malaysia’s facilities and sold in the Malaysian market.\n...\nIs KitKat Chunky halal? KitKat Chunky UK is not halal certified, following the same status as standard UK KitKat. KitKat Chunky Malaysia is halal certified under JAKIM. The product name is the same, but the certificationstatus depends on country of manufacture."], 'image': 'https://halalspy.com/og-default.png'}, {'id': 'https://thehalallife.co.uk/products/kitkat/', 'title': 'KitKat – The Halal Life', 'url': 'https://thehalallife.co.uk/products/kitkat/', 'highlights': ['KitKat – The Halal Life\n\nThis brand has been listed as a Boycott Brand by the Witness. You can read more information here: Nestle | Israel Boycott Guide | by The Witness\n\nThis page relates to KitKat Bar, a Nestle product, Last updated March 2024. Every effort has been made to assess each Nestle’s product individually.\n\nNestle produces a Suitable for Vegetarian list, so some information is based on the list they provide. All products on this list are free from meat, fish and their derivatives. Nestle confirm various ingredients are used around the world and there is an increasing number of importers of foreign nestle products in the UK. So they cannot guarantee your product is 100% suitable, you will need to check the pack to make sure it is a UK product. Nestle’s continuously are adding and removing their products from their shelves, if you want us to ask about a specific product. Leave a message below.\n\nThe following are halal and also suitable for Vegetarians:\n\nHalal\n\n2 Finger\n\n- Cookies Cream\n- Dark\n- Orange\n- Dark Mint\n- Lemon Drizzle\n- Original\n- Contact\n\n4Finger\n\n- Ruby\n- Dark\n- Original\n\nChunky\n\n- Peanut Butter\n- Salted Caramel\n- More Chunky\n- Original\n\nKitKat Senses\n\n- Double Choclate\n- Hazlenut\n- Salted Caramel\n\nKitKat Ruby\n\n- KitKat Ruby\n\nRecipes may change so always check the ingredients on the pack.'], 'favicon': 'https://thehalallife.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/cropped-HLLogo-2-270x270.jpg'}, {'id': 'https://halalcodecheck.com/blog/is-kitkat-halal/', 'title': 'Is KitKat Halal? UK, US & Malaysia Verdict (2026)', 'url': 'https://halalcodecheck.com/blog/is-kitkat-halal/', 'highlights': ['KitKat in the UK is Mushbooh — no halal certification and E322 source unverified. Malaysia and Turkey KitKat are JAKIM-certified halal. Full regional breakdown.\n...\nShare:\n\n \n\n Shopping Guides #chocolate #shopping-guide #e-codes #mushbooh #halal-certification \n\nKitKat in the UK is Mushbooh — no halal certification, and the source of its emulsifiers is not confirmed on the label. In Malaysia and Turkey, KitKat is JAKIM-certified halal and fully permissible. If you are in the UK or US and require certification, you should choose a certified alternative.\n...\n| Market | Manufacturer | Halal Certified | Certifying Body | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n...\n| UK | Nestlé UK | No | — | Mushbooh |\n...\n| US | Hershey (Nestlé licence) | No | — | Mushbooh |\n...\n| Malaysia | Nestlé Malaysia | Yes | JAKIM | Halal |\n...\n| Turkey | Nestlé Turkey | Yes | Local halal body | Halal |\n...\n| UAE / Gulf | Nestlé Middle East | Varies by product | — | Check packaging |\n...\n023.\n...\ncertified KitKat\n...\n| Question | Answer |\n| --- | --- |\n| Does KitKat contain pork? | No listed pork ingredients |\n|Is UK KitKat halal-certified? | No — certification ended in 2023 |\n| Is E322 in KitKat halal? | Likely soy-derived, but unconfirmed without cert |\n| Is E476 in KitKat halal? | Yes — plant-derived (castor oil) |\n| Is E442 in KitKat halal? | Yes — generally accepted as halal |\n| Is Malaysian KitKat halal? | Yes — JAKIM certified |\n| Overall UK verdict | Mushbooh |\n...\nThe verdict for UK and US KitKat is Mushbooh — not because of known haram ingredients, but because halal certification is absent and emulsifier sourcing cannot be confirmed from the label. For those who require certification, Malaysian or Turkish KitKat or certified alternatives are the appropriate choice.\n...\nIn the UK and US, this depends on your personal standard. If you require halal certification for all chocolate, UK KitKat does not meet that standard. If you accept products without known haram ingredients and judge the likely soy source of E322 as sufficient, that is a personal and scholarly matter. Malaysian KitKat is certified halal and fully permissible under any standard.\n...\nIs KitKat halal in the\n...\n@type":"\n...\n","text":"KitKat in\n...\n@type":"Question","name":"What E-codes are in KitKat?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"UK KitKat contains E322 (lecithin — usually soy, source unconfirmed without certification), E442 (ammonium phosphatides — derived from soy lecithin and ammonium, generally considered halal), and E476 (PGPR — derived from castor oil, plant-based, halal). The chocolate coating also contains milk, whey, and sugar\n...\n@type":"Question","name":"Is KitKat suitable for Muslims?\n...\nKitKat production?'], 'image': 'https://halalcodecheck.com/blog/images/is-kitkat-halal.webp'}, {'id': 'https://halalcodecheck.com/brand/kitkat/', 'title': '', 'url': 'https://halalcodecheck.com/brand/kitkat/', 'highlights': ['---\ntitle: Is Kit Kat Halal? Complete Guide | HalalCodeCheck\ndescription: Is Kit Kat halal? Standard UK and US Kit Kat has no halal certification. It contains E476 (PGPR) and E322 (lecitstlé produces halal-certified Kit Kat for Malaysia, Indonesia, and some Gulf markets.\nimage: https://halalcodecheck.com/brand/images/kitkat.webp\n...\n![Is Kit Kat Halal? — brand guide](https://halalcodecheck.com/brand/images/kitkat.webp) \n\n# Is Kit Kat Halal?\n\n ⚠️ Mushbooh \n\nKit Kat sold in the UK and US contains E476 (PGPR — polyglycerol polyricinoleate) from an unverified glycerol source, making it Mushbooh. E322 is listed as soya lecithin (halal). No halal certification on standard UK/US products. Nestlé holds halal certification for Kit Kat produced in Malaysia and Indonesia.\n...\nNo halal certification on standard UK or US products. Halal-certified Kit Kat manufactured in Malaysia (JAKIM) and Indonesia (MUI). Some Gulf market products carry regional certification.\n...\nStandard Kit Kat sold in the UK and US **does not carryhalal certification**. The bar contains no pork or alcohol, and the wafer is made from wheat flour and vegetable fat. The primary concern is the **chocolate coating**, which contains emulsifiers whose sources are not independently verified.\n...\nThe ricinoleate portion is halal. The concern is the **glycerol source** — if from animal tallow (non-halal slaughter), the E476 is Mushbooh or haram. Nestlé has not publicly confirmed the glycerol source for its UK Kit Kat.\n...\nUK Kit Kat specifies “soya lecithin” — plant-derived, considered halal. This is a positive indicator but does not substitute for full halal certification.\n...\nCritically:\n...\nMalaysian-produced KitKat only\n...\nKit Kat bar was not produced under\n...\n| Factor | Details |\n| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Halal certification (UK/US) | None |\n| Key concern | E476 — glycerol source unverified |\n| E322 source | Soya (halal) — specified on UK label |\n| Verdict | Mushbooh — not verifiably halal without certification |\n| Halal versions | Available in Malaysia (JAKIM), Indonesia (MUI), and some Gulf markets |\n| Recommendation | Purchase from halal retailers who stock Malaysian/Indonesian Kit Kat, or choose a certified alternative |\n...\nStandard UK and US Kit Kat cannot be confirmed as halal. Look for the JAKIM or MUI logo if purchasing in those markets, or choose a fully halal-certified chocolate bar.\n...\nIM-certified Malaysian KitKat and MUI-certified Indonesian KitKat\n...\nhalal across all\n...\n(Vegan) 4 Finger\n...\n-only ingredients\n...\nis acceptable across all']}]}


